<div class="article-body">

<p>Short version first: if your hydroponic lettuce roots are brown and slimy, you have Pythium root rot, and the cause is almost never your nutrients. It is warm water and low oxygen. Pull one plant out and squeeze a single root between two fingers. If the outer skin slips off and leaves a thin white core behind, you have the rat-tail, and that is Pythium.</p>

<p>I lost a full tray of butterhead to it. Six heads. Ten weeks of work, gone in nine days. I did what everyone does and blamed the nutrients. Cal-mag for $22. Bloom feed for $18. The roots kept turning to brown soup and the leaves kept wilting at 2pm anyway.</p>

<p>Root rot does not start when the leaves wilt. By the time you see that wilt, it has been running for a week. That gap is the whole problem, and it is why every fix I tried first was aimed at the wrong week.</p>

<p>I am not a plant pathologist. I grow lettuce on a shelf in a closet. But I have now killed one tray with this and saved three, and the difference came down to two numbers nobody told me to measure: water temperature and dissolved oxygen.</p>

<h2 id="what">1. What Root Rot Actually Is (A Drowning, Not a Disease)</h2>

<p>Pythium is not a fungus. It is an oomycete, a water mold, which is why the fungicide logic people bring to it never works. It releases spores with little tails that swim through your nutrient solution and steer toward root tips that are already damaged or short on oxygen.</p>

<p>On lettuce specifically, two species do most of the damage: <strong>Pythium aphanidermatum</strong> and <strong>Pythium dissotocum</strong>. That comes straight from the UConn e-GRO bulletin on Pythium root rot in hydroponic lettuce, which is the clearest thing I have read on the topic.</p>

<p>Here is the part most guides skip. You will never keep Pythium out of a home system. It rides in on tap water, on seedlings, on your hands, on reused net cups, and on fungus gnats. The goal is not a sterile reservoir, because you cannot have one. The goal is roots strong enough that the pathogen never gets the upper hand.</p>

<p>And that reframes the whole job. You are not curing a sick plant. You are keeping a plant out of a fight it cannot win.</p>

<div class="callout">
  <div class="callout-title">The Damage You Cannot See</div>
  <p>In a UConn trial, lettuce infected with Pythium that showed <strong>zero visible symptoms</strong> still weighed an average of 24 grams less than healthy plants. That is a whole serving of salad missing from a plant that looks fine. If a head is growing slower than its neighbor, the roots already know why.</p>
</div>

<h2 id="signs">2. The Three Signs, and the One Test That Decides</h2>

<p>Root rot moves in order. Catch it at stage one and the plant usually comes back. Catch it at stage three and that plant is done.</p>

<ul>
  <li><strong>Stage one:</strong> brown spots on the root tips, fewer fine root hairs, roots look thinner than they should.</li>
  <li><strong>Stage two:</strong> roots go brown and soft, and the outer layer slips off when you pull a root gently between two fingers, leaving a thin stringy core. Growers call this the rat-tail.</li>
  <li><strong>Stage three:</strong> roots turn slimy and dark brown to black, and they smell sour. Not earthy. Sour, like something died in the water. Because something did.</li>
</ul>

<p><strong>The test that decides it:</strong> pull one plant and drag a single root between your thumb and finger. Tan roots that stay whole are fine. Roots where the skin slides off and leaves a hair-thin core are infected.</p>

<p>Color alone will fool you. Older roots naturally fade to cream or tan, and some nutrient additives stain roots brown without hurting anything. So do not diagnose from color. Go by feel and smell. Firm roots with a clean smell are healthy at any shade. Soft, slimy roots that peel and stink are not.</p>

<p><strong>Here is the key insight:</strong> the water tells you before the leaves do. Above the water, the first sign is subtle. The lettuce wilts in the warm part of the afternoon and perks back up in the evening, even though the water level is fine. Growth stalls. You may get tip burn or yellowing, because failing roots cannot move water and calcium up the plant. By the time the plant looks like it is dying all day, the roots have been gone for days.</p>

<h2 id="water">3. Why Warm Water Is the Whole Story</h2>

<p>Roots do not drink from the water. They breathe it. Roots need oxygen dissolved in the solution to burn the sugars they were fed, and that dissolved oxygen is the first thing warm water steals.</p>

<p>The squeeze runs both directions at once. As water warms, it holds less oxygen. At the same time, the roots respire faster, so they want more of it. Less supply, more demand, in the same afternoon.</p>

