  </thead>
  <tbody>
    <tr><td>68F (20C)</td><td>9.1 mg/L</td><td>Sweet spot. Roots happy, low rot pressure.</td></tr>
    <tr><td>72F (22C)</td><td>8.7 mg/L</td><td>Upper edge for lettuce. Watch it from here.</td></tr>
    <tr><td>75F (24C)</td><td>8.4 mg/L</td><td>The line in the sand. See section 4.</td></tr>
    <tr><td>79F (26C)</td><td>8.1 mg/L</td><td>Risk zone. This is where mine lived.</td></tr>
    <tr><td>86F (30C)</td><td>7.6 mg/L</td><td>Emergency. Submerged roots suffocating.</td></tr>
  </tbody>
</table>

<p>Those saturation numbers come from the USGS dissolved oxygen equations, and they are a ceiling, not a promise. Your reservoir only sits near them if aeration keeps up with what the roots and microbes consume. A warm, crowded bucket with one tired air stone runs well below its own ceiling.</p>

<p>Below about 4 to 5 mg/L, roots stop respiring efficiently and start to leak. That leak is the open door Pythium swims through. So the target is <strong>6 mg/L minimum, ideally 8 to 9</strong>. Cornell's Neil Mattson recommends aerating above 6 ppm, and a University of Kentucky e-GRO alert notes that saturated dissolved oxygen around 8 ppm can cut Pythium spore survival.</p>

<p><strong>Pay attention to this number: 72F.</strong> For lettuce, 65 to 72F is the working band. Every degree above it is a loss on both sides of the problem, less oxygen in the water and more appetite for it in the roots.</p>

<p>Two more things feed the same fire. Stagnant water, because Pythium zoospores need only a slow current to find a root tip. And algae on your channel surfaces and reservoir lid, because fungus gnats and shore flies graze on that algae and then carry Pythium from pot to pot.</p>

<p>Even your light is part of the water math. A panel or bar two feet above an open reservoir adds a couple of degrees by hour six of the photoperiod. If you are comparing lights and wondering which one runs coolest over an open tank, the <a href="/grow-lights">Grow Light DB</a> lists the actual measured draw for each one.</p>

<h2 id="twist">4. The 75F Number That Tricks Everyone</h2>

<p>This is where it gets interesting. I read the UConn guide properly and found the sentence that had been quietly wrecking my diagnosis for months. It says the optimum nutrient-solution temperature for lettuce is 75F (24C).</p>

<p>My closet reservoir was running 79F. If 75F is the optimum, I was barely off it, and temperature could not possibly be my problem. Right?</p>

<p>Wrong, and here is the contradiction that took me two dead trays to unwind. Every commercial greenhouse guide says 75F is the growth sweet spot for lettuce. Every home guide, mine included now, says keep it under 72F or Pythium wins. Both are true, because they are describing two different systems.</p>

<p>In a commercial greenhouse, the system is disinfected between cycles and the water is treated continuously. At 75F, lettuce grows fastest and Pythium never finds a foothold. In a closet where nothing gets disinfected and your net cups have been reused three times, 75F is simply the line where the pathogen outruns the plant.</p>

<p>The number is not wrong. It is conditional. For a home system, 75F is the ceiling, not the target.</p>

<p>And that is exactly why the peroxide bottle and the cal-mag bottle never fixed anything for me. Both treat the water. Neither touches the temperature. The temperature is what keeps handing Pythium a fresh invitation every single afternoon.</p>

<h2 id="rescue">5. The Rescue That Actually Worked</h2>

<p>When it came back in my second batch, I already knew what it was. Here is the order that matters.</p>

<p><strong>Step 1, pull and cut.</strong> Lift every plant. Trim the brown and slimy roots back to white, firm tissue with clean scissors. Rinse the root mass under cool water. If the whole root ball is black and soupy, bin that plant. A dead plant sitting in the reservoir is a spore factory for every plant sharing the water. And for the record: root rot does not make lettuce toxic. If a plant recovers and the new leaves are crisp, they are fine to eat. Anything slimy is not food.</p>

<p><strong>Step 2, chill the water.</strong> You cannot fix what you cannot see, so get a <a href="https://www.amazon.com/s?k=waterproof+digital+thermometer+with+probe+for+liquid&tag=tabletopharve-20" target="_blank" rel="nofollow sponsored">waterproof digital thermometer with a probe</a> into the reservoir before anything else. Then drop two frozen 1-liter bottles in. In my closet that took 79F to 68F and held it for about ten hours. Where the tank sits matters too: off the floor, away from the light, off any heat mat.</p>

<p><strong>Step 3, add oxygen.</strong> Swap the single tiny stone for a <a href="https://www.amazon.com/s?k=aquarium+air+pump+dual+outlet+with+air+stone&tag=tabletopharve-20" target="_blank" rel="nofollow sponsored">dual-outlet air pump with air stones</a> and let it move the surface. My first stone was a 1-inch disc that barely rippled the water. The 4-inch stone with a second outlet changed the whole tank. Surface agitation is the cheapest oxygen you will ever buy.</p>

<p><strong>Step 4, reset the water.</strong> A bottle of <a href="https://www.amazon.com/s?k=hydrogen+peroxide+3+percent&tag=tabletopharve-20" target="_blank" rel="nofollow sponsored">3% hydrogen peroxide</a> at roughly 3 ml per liter of reservoir water disinfests the solution as it breaks down into water and oxygen. It is a reset, not a cure, and it flat kills the good biology along with the bad. If you would rather build a colony than nuke the tank, the same slot is filled by <a href="https://www.amazon.com/s?k=beneficial+bacteria+for+hydroponics+root+rot&tag=tabletopharve-20" target="_blank" rel="nofollow sponsored">beneficial bacteria for hydroponics</a>. You run one or the other. Not both.</p>

<p><strong>Step 5, sanitize between cycles.</strong> Empty the system, scrub off every bit of old plant matter, then apply your sanitizer and rinse it out. The scrubbing is the part that matters, not the chemical. Organic residue protects the spores from whatever you pour on it.</p>

<p>Batch two, before any of this: 4 of 6 heads lost. Batch three, with a $14 thermometer, a second air stone, and two frozen bottles a day: 6 of 6 harvested, and the roots stayed white for 44 days.</p>

<div class="callout">
  <div class="callout-title">Quick Math</div>
  <p>A 1-inch air stone moves almost nothing. A 4-inch stone on a dual-outlet pump costs about $20 total and raises dissolved oxygen across the whole tank. One wasted tray of six lettuce heads is roughly $12 in seed and ten weeks of your patience. The pump pays for itself the first time it saves a batch.</p>
</div>

<h2 id="prevent">6. Stopping the Next Outbreak</h2>

<p>So now you have the diagnosis and the rescue. But the real question is how you stop doing this every July.</p>

<p>There are two lanes and you have to pick one. <strong>Lane A is sterile:</strong> peroxide or a hypochlorous acid product, topped up every few days, killing everything in the water on a schedule. It is cheap and it works. <strong>Lane B is biological:</strong> a beneficial bacteria product that colonises the roots and out-competes Pythium. It is slower to establish and more forgiving once it does.</p>

<p>The mistake is mixing them. Peroxide kills the colony you just paid for, so you end up in lane A with lane B's price tag, and no protection from either.</p>

<p>Neither lane saves you at 80F. The temperature and oxygen work has to come first, because that is what makes either lane hold.</p>

<p>The honest sequence is boring: measure the water temp, keep it 65 to 72F, keep dissolved oxygen above 6, and clean the system between cycles. The dramatic rescue only exists because the boring part got skipped.</p>

<p><strong>Here is the honest truth.</strong> The cheap dual-outlet air pump is loud. Mine buzzed through the shelf hard enough that I sat it on a folded towel, and if your rig lives in a bedroom you will hear it at night. A single-outlet pump is quieter but you are back to one stone. I chose the noise.</p>

<p><strong>Bottom line:</strong> if your lettuce roots are already brown, buy the two cheapest things first, a <a href="https://www.amazon.com/s?k=waterproof+digital+thermometer+with+probe+for+liquid&tag=tabletopharve-20" target="_blank" rel="nofollow sponsored">water thermometer</a> and a bigger <a href="https://www.amazon.com/s?k=aquarium+air+pump+dual+outlet+with+air+stone&tag=tabletopharve-20" target="_blank" rel="nofollow sponsored">air pump</a>. The thermometer was $14 when I last checked and bounces between $12 and $18, so under $15 is a solid deal. The pump runs $18 to $25. Add a quart of <a href="https://www.amazon.com/s?k=hydrogen+peroxide+3+percent&tag=tabletopharve-20" target="_blank" rel="nofollow sponsored">3% hydrogen peroxide</a> for about $8 and you have the entire rescue for under $45. That is less than one dead tray.</p>

<p><strong>Read next:</strong> if the roots look white and the plant is still stalling, this is not your problem. Start with <a href="/posts/hydroponic-lettuce-not-growing">why hydroponic lettuce stops growing</a>, then check <a href="/posts/hydroponic-water-temperature">reservoir water temperature</a> and the general <a href="/posts/hydroponic-root-rot-fix">hydroponic root rot fix guide</a> for the non-lettuce version.</p>

